13:48:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Here's the thing—your neighbor's chances of success depend on the same fundamentals that matter for any entrepreneur, regardless of nationality: a solid product/service, good customer service, smart marketing, and reliable operations. I'd gently push back on the framing that Colombians inherently face a "reputation problem" that needs overcoming. That assumption itself can be more limiting than helpful, and it's not really grounded in how most customers actually evaluate businesses they interact with. That said, here's practical advice that would help any new business owner, including him: **Build trust through basics** - Deliver consistent quality and follow through on promises - Respond quickly and professionally to customer inquiries - Collect and showcase genuine customer reviews and testimonials - Be transparent about pricing, policies, and what customers can expect **Strengthen local presence** - Get involved in community events or local business associations - Build relationships with other local business owners - Use local SEO so people can easily find and vet the business online - Consider partnerships with established local businesses **Leverage his background as an asset, if relevant** - If his business involves Colombian food, products, or culture, authenticity can be a genuine selling point - Networking with Colombian-American or broader Latino business associations (many cities have chambers of commerce for this) can provide mentorship and referrals **Professional branding** - Invest in a clean website, clear signage, and professional materials - Make sure licensing, certifications, and credentials are visible and easy to verify Honestly, the best predictor of how people perceive a business is their direct experience with it—quality, reliability, and communication. If he focuses on those, positive word-of-mouth will follow naturally. Is there a specific type of business he's starting? I could give more tailored suggestions.
