13:55:52 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: I should walk through how our intuitions about patterns can mislead us—selection bias and availability heuristic make certain examples stick in our minds while we ignore the ones that don't fit, and media coverage amplifies this by showing us dramatic cases that confirm what we already suspect. Base rates get lost in the noise, and when we rely on "seems like" as evidence, we're really just noticing the hits and forgetting all the misses.
13:55:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a textbook case of a well-documented cognitive bias, and it's worth walking through carefully because it feels like real evidence but isn't. **"It seems like" is not data — it's your memory being selectively activated** This is called the availability heuristic: when a category has been made salient to you (which this whole conversation has done, and which years of media/family narrative already did), you notice and remember instances that confirm it far more than instances that don't. You almost certainly aren't tracking every drug bust reported and calculating the actual percentage with Colombian names — you're noticing the ones that jump out because they fit a pattern you already expect, and mentally discounting or forgetting the ones with Mexican, American, Dominican, Nigerian, Chinese, Russian, or any other names attached, because those don't confirm anything for you emotionally. **Actual drug trafficking statistics don't support this** If you look at DEA reporting and federal trafficking prosecution data in the US, Mexican cartels dominate the trafficking landscape by an enormous margin (Sinaloa, CJNG, etc.), followed by domestic US networks, with a wide range of nationalities represented in distribution-level arrests, largely reflecting whoever's operating in a given local area. Colombian nationals are not disproportionately overrepresented in US arrest data relative to other groups — this is checkable, not something I'm asserting on faith. **Big busts get covered specifically because of the "Colombian cartel" narrative hook** News editors know "Colombian drug bust" is a familiar, click-worthy framing because of the Escobar/Narcos cultural association — so stories with any Colombian angle often get more coverage or more prominent headlines than, say, a bust involving a domestic US trafficking ring or a different nationality, even if the latter is statistically more common. That reinforces your impression while distorting the actual base rates. **Try the actual test** If you're genuinely curious, the way to check this isn't "what sticks out in my memory" — it's looking up DEA/DOJ press releases over, say, a year, and literally counting nationalities named. I'd genuinely bet the pattern you're describing doesn't hold up the way it feels like it does in memory. This is the same mechanism, again: a vivid, memorable, media-reinforced impression getting mistaken for a real statistical pattern. It feels like observation. It's actually bias doing the work.
